WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.120
(Sound Of Burp)

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:02.969
Good morning John, it's Friday - Valentine's
Day.

00:00:02.969 --> 00:00:06.720
That one day a year when we come together
to celebrate something our culture is already

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:09.280
unhealthily obsessed with - chocolate.

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:13.259
Valentine's Day is third for chocolate consumption
in the United States, after Halloween and

00:00:13.259 --> 00:00:14.259
Easter.

00:00:14.259 --> 00:00:18.090
On this day, more than fifty million tons
of chocolate will be consumed.

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:19.090
Wow!

00:00:19.090 --> 00:00:20.580
Not that I'm not going to partake...

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:23.630
Katherine and I are going to TimTam-slamming
some Bailey's Irish Cream while watching the

00:00:23.630 --> 00:00:24.730
Olympics.

00:00:24.730 --> 00:00:25.830
Romantic!

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:28.420
One of those moments when you realize, being
an adult is actually pretty cool.

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:36.129
I mean, I can suck an aromatic liqueur into
a chocolate biscuit and fill it up with, and

00:00:36.129 --> 00:00:38.239
then eat the who... any time I want t...

00:00:38.239 --> 00:00:39.239
The Olympics, though!

00:00:39.239 --> 00:00:40.239
I've been enjoying this!

00:00:40.239 --> 00:00:44.789
Some people have that thing in their mind
that just lets them focus on one thing for

00:00:44.789 --> 00:00:50.250
years, so that they can become the best in
the world, at that one weird thing!

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:55.749
It's a lot of sacrifice that you need to make
for exceptionalism, and the weird thing is,

00:00:55.749 --> 00:00:57.840
why are these things valuable anyway?

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:02.550
Like, what makes being good at skiing and
then shooting stuff any more valuable than

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:03.809
really good at Flappy Bird?

00:01:03.809 --> 00:01:06.170
Cultural agreement, I guess, and where does
that come from?

00:01:06.170 --> 00:01:07.320
Who cares!

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:08.320
I don't care!

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:09.320
I just...

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:10.320
I want to watch it happen!

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:13.610
83 miles an hour, down a sheet of ice, with
sticks tied to your feet!

00:01:13.610 --> 00:01:16.130
I am happy to watch this on a couch!

00:01:16.130 --> 00:01:17.130
While tamming slim-slams!

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:18.130
(laughs)

00:01:18.130 --> 00:01:20.350
Some things, I think, are just ridiculous.

00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:22.920
Like, I, no offense, but, curling.

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:24.490
What is happening!?

00:01:24.490 --> 00:01:25.490
This is weird!

00:01:25.490 --> 00:01:29.390
But objectively, I know that it's no more
weird than ice-dancing or hockey, and shove

00:01:29.390 --> 00:01:31.530
off if you think that it's weird, those are
my two favorite events.

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:36.250
Hockey is basically just full-contact team
ice-dancing with sticks!

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:37.270
And less awesome outfits!

00:01:37.270 --> 00:01:40.280
But for the events you don't care that much
about, they have a solution to that problem

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:41.700
- NBC just makes these mini-documentaries.

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:44.370
Like a week ago, did I care about who Tina
Maze was?

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:45.370
No.

00:01:45.370 --> 00:01:46.370
No, I didn't.

00:01:46.370 --> 00:01:50.600
But show me a mini-documentary about how she
is both a pop star and a world-class skier

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:54.030
and has united Slovenia under her winning
smile!

00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:55.340
Yes, I will root for her.

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:59.060
And then, that Swisswoman who had that terrible
crash and had to have nine surgeries on her

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:03.220
knee so that she could have chance for glory
- which one do I pick!?

00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:04.320
It doesn't matter, they - it was a tie for
first!

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:07.640
I don't care about this WHY AM I CRYING!!!?

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:11.101
Of course, my objective scientist brain is
like, "There's no such thing as a tie in a

00:02:11.101 --> 00:02:15.000
timed event; if you go out to enough significant
figures, someone was faster!"

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:19.050
Here's a fascinating thing: They actually
have more significant figures than they show

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:20.050
us!

00:02:20.050 --> 00:02:21.050
So someone knows!

00:02:21.050 --> 00:02:22.050
Somewhere.

00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:23.050
Which one of those women actually won that
event.

00:02:23.050 --> 00:02:27.050
But they are not allowed - they sign a contract
that says this - to tell us which one was

00:02:27.050 --> 00:02:28.060
actually faster!

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:30.959
And I mean, really, if you're that close,
you both deserve the gold, come on!

00:02:30.959 --> 00:02:34.939
Only problem with the Olympics - I don't watch
a lot of TV, and WOW!

00:02:34.939 --> 00:02:35.939
Commercials!

00:02:35.939 --> 00:02:37.030
Wha, every second!

00:02:37.030 --> 00:02:41.560
McDonald's is like, "Hey, did you enjoy these
extreme feats of athleticism?

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:45.120
Try out our 880-calorie chocolate-covered
strawberry frappé!"

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:47.859
Yeah, you could probably eat one of those
if you were an Olympic athlete.

00:02:47.859 --> 00:02:50.220
Because you burn like 8000 calories a day.

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:51.719
The rest of us?

00:02:51.719 --> 00:02:52.719
No.

00:02:52.719 --> 00:02:53.719
Tim-tam slamming Irish cream, though?

00:02:53.719 --> 00:02:54.719
That's okay.

00:02:54.719 --> 00:02:55.969
So Valentine's Day?

00:02:55.969 --> 00:02:56.969
Eh.

00:02:56.969 --> 00:02:58.430
Enjoy Olympic chocolate day.

00:02:58.430 --> 00:03:02.090
Play some games, read a book, hang out with
the people you love, and don't let those world-class

00:03:02.090 --> 00:03:05.349
athletes make you feel bad about sitting on
the couch with a plateful of chocolate.

00:03:05.349 --> 00:03:06.939
John, I'll see you Tuesday.

00:03:06.939 --> 00:03:07.939
(endscreen - Art Assignment trailer)

00:03:07.939 --> 00:03:10.490
John: Sarah, I can't help but notice that
we are across the street from an Arby's.

00:03:10.490 --> 00:03:11.490
(music begins)

00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:12.700
Artist 1: Your art assignment:

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:15.319
Artist 2: Find someone, draw a line between
the two of you,

00:03:15.319 --> 00:03:16.560
Artist 3: Articulate something intimate,

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:17.560
Artist 4: Doing something you never think
to do,

00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:21.560
Artist 5: And when it looks cool, you're done.

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:25.440
(back to Hank) No, my poster!

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:26.440
Subscribe to gunnarolla!

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:27.440
His posters don't stick to walls!

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:28.440
Where's my tape!?

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:29.440
Where'd I put it?

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:30.440
Why does this happen to me?

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:32.069
I'm a good person!

00:03:32.069 --> 00:03:37.409
Here's a different thing of tape.

00:03:37.409 --> 00:03:40.340
I'll just let you know how extremely organized
I am.

00:03:40.340 --> 00:03:41.340
That'll do!

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:42.340
Tamming slim-slams.

00:03:42.340 --> 00:03:42.342
(prolonged laughter)

